While the linear increase of nucleoid length with cell length is the result of equilibrium osmotic pressure balance, the splitting of the nucleoid is entirely due to outof-equilibrium processes. In fact, for cells with ρtot = 2400/μm and a half length of ∼ 4 μm or larger, the equilibrium steady state used as the initial condition for the reaction-diffusion equations yields a nucleoid with a single lobe. By contrast, the nucleoid splits into two identical lobes positioned at 1/4 and 3/4 of the long cell axis when the reaction-diffusion Eqs. [1]–[3] are integrated forward in time, see Fig. 2 and Movie S1. Such a 1/4 and 3/4 positioning of the daughter nucleoids has been ubiquitously observed in experiments [10].
